{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And you shall make</b> again <b>two gold rings, and you shall put them on the two shoulder-pieces underneath, opposite its front.</b> That is, on the back side of the high priest, at the lower edges of the shoulder-pieces that are attached, opposite the upper edge of the ephod — which is the front of the ephod; this is <b>opposite its front.</b> One may say that the ephod was on the front and back of the high priest like a short garment that they call corsete, for how is it possible that it be from his waist downward, as a garment there — we do not find a garment of this sort except for breeches, to cover the flesh of nakedness, and moreover the threads are doubled to twenty-eight, as Rashi explained, and it is very heavy, and if so, it would impede the legs of the priest performing the service; and also, when the priest bends to the sides, the shoulder-pieces slip off his shoulders. Therefore one may say that it is from his waist upward to the height of his shoulders, like the measure of his other garments." "<b>Opposite its front.</b> On the outer side of the ephod, not on the inner side that is attached to the back of the priest. And by saying of the breastpiece 'at its house,' he says here of the ephod 'opposite its front.'" "And in what place on the shoulder-pieces shall you place these rings? <b>Opposite its joining</b> — opposite the place where the ephod is joined to the shoulder-pieces by its sewing with the band <b>above the band of the ephod.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "קורשיט״א rendered as Old French 'corsete' (a short tunic). The comment cites Rashi by name.", 1 "The phrase 'ביתה' ('at its house') refers to the term used for the breastpiece's attachment in v. 23."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:59.976284031Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}